Source catalog
Every upstream file is declared in config/nhanes_files.csv. The catalog records:

the official release cycle and file identifier;
the direct CDC HTTPS URL;
expected row count;
record level and merge key;
exam and analytic role.

An upstream row-count change stops the pipeline so the CDC revision can be reviewed instead of silently changing a research dataset.

Survey design and weights
NHANES is a complex, multistage probability sample. Valid population estimates generally require:

the weight appropriate to the least inclusive analytic component;
strata (SDMVSTRA) and primary sampling units (SDMVPSU);
adjusted weights when compatible cycles are combined;
domain-analysis methods rather than deleting sampled records prematurely;
review of eligibility, nonresponse, and laboratory-method changes.

This pipeline preserves design variables but does not select a universal weight, because the correct choice depends on each analysis. Consult the NHANES weighting tutorial before estimating population quantities.

Provenance and validation
Each run produces:

provenance.csv, containing source URLs, retrieval time, byte size, SHA-256, and imported dimensions;
validation.csv, containing output dimensions, participant counts, and duplicate-participant counts;
compressed RDS datasets, with CSV copies only when requested.

CI runs synthetic tests on every pull request. A separate scheduled workflow performs the network-dependent CDC refresh so temporary upstream availability does not make ordinary code review unreliable.

Expanding to more NHANES components
Adding catalog rows is only the acquisition step. Each new component also needs:

record-level and key validation;
a variable harmonization crosswalk;
eligibility and missing-data documentation;
survey-weight guidance;
tests for every merge or reshape;
review of differences across survey cycles.

This separation makes the repository extensible without implying that all NHANES files can be safely combined through one generic join.
